INT. UNDERGROUND PARKING LEVEL P4 - NIGHT

Concrete pillars. Humming strip lights. One car left: a silver sedan with its trunk open.

NADEZHDA VOLKOVA-REYNOLDS, 30s, trench coat, crouches by the rear wheel. A phone in her hand, held flat like a tray. Her breath fogs.

She thumbs the screen. A voice memo plays. Her own voice, recorded earlier, calm and clipped.

NADEZHDA (V.O.)
If you are hearing this, level four is clear. Listen carefully. Do not skip.

NADEZHDA
(to the phone)
I'm not skipping.

NADEZHDA (V.O.)
Check the trunk.

She stands. Scans the pillars. Slowly. Crosses to the sedan and looks in. Her face tightens.

NADEZHDA
Empty.

NADEZHDA (V.O.)
Check again.

She lifts the felt lining. Under it, a small black flash drive taped to the floor. She peels it free.

NADEZHDA
Got it.

NADEZHDA (V.O.)
Good. Now walk to the stairwell. Do not use the elevator.

She stops. Looks at the elevator. Its numbers are climbing: 1. 2. 3.

NADEZHDA
Why not the elevator?

NADEZHDA (V.O.)
Because I know what I know, and you do not.

NADEZHDA
That's not an answer.

NADEZHDA (V.O.)
It is the only one I left you.

The numbers climb. 4. A ping. The doors slide open on an empty car.

She backs away, heels clicking on concrete. Footsteps start somewhere behind the pillars. More than one pair. Unhurried.

NADEZHDA
(whisper)
They're here.

NADEZHDA (V.O.)
Do not turn around.

She freezes. A ceiling camera swivels, tracking her.

NADEZHDA
I recorded this at two a.m. I was drunk.

NADEZHDA (V.O.)
You were sober. You were terrified. Walk.

She drops the phone. Stamps it into splinters. Sprints for the stairwell, shoulders the steel door, and it slams behind her.

INT. STAIRWELL - CONTINUOUS

Bare concrete. A flight up, a flight down. She stops. Breathing hard. Listens.

Below, a door opens. Above, a door opens.

NADEZHDA
Okay. Okay. Think.

She turns the flash drive over. A tiny label, handwritten, in her own script. She reads it. Her eyes widen.

NADEZHDA (CONT'D)
No. No, no.

Footsteps from both directions, converging. She backs against the wall and slides down until she sits, the drive clutched to her chest.

A cracked air vent above the landing. She notices it. Stares.

NADEZHDA (CONT'D)
Of course it's a vent.

She climbs onto the rail. Tests the grille. It gives. She hauls herself up and in, scraping her coat, legs vanishing just as two pairs of boots reach the landing below.

INT. VENT SHAFT - CONTINUOUS

Tight metal. Dust. Her breath loud in the tube. She crawls on elbows, the drive in her teeth.

Far behind, voices echo, too muffled to parse. She keeps moving.

Light ahead. A grille. She presses her face to it.

EXT. LOADING DOCK - CONTINUOUS

Rain. A van idling, rear door ajar.

She kicks the grille out. It clangs on the concrete below. She drops after it, rolls, comes up on one knee.

The van is empty. On the passenger seat, a second phone, glowing.

It rings.

She stares at it. Rain runs off her chin. She steps forward and answers.

NADEZHDA
Who is this?

NADEZHDA (V.O.)
(through the phone, her own voice)
Someone who needed you to find the drive.

NADEZHDA
That's my voice.

NADEZHDA (V.O.)
Run.

The line dies. Behind her the dock lights snap on, white and blinding.

She raises the drive above her head.

NADEZHDA (CONT'D)
(shouting at the light)
Then come and take it!

Silence. The rain falls. The light holds.
